{
 "_about": "Pape Avenue south of Gerrard is not in streets.geojson, so the relief lines use points on its line extended south (crossings with Gerrard, Queen and Eastern computed from it). Transit history timeline page (engine/history/build.py), at docs/toronto/ttc-history/. Each line has a geometry recipe and a dated list of events. Geometry recipes: {\"line\": id, \"from\": station, \"to\": station} cuts a published subway_lines.geojson line between two stations; {\"build\": id} is a line from construction.geojson; {\"along\": [streets], \"from\": pt, \"to\": pt} follows the named streets in streets.geojson; {\"path\": [pt, ...]} joins points with straight lines; {\"parts\": [...]} chains recipes. A pt is a station name in subway_stations.geojson, \"build:<id>:<name>\" (a construction.geojson station), {\"x\": [street, street]} (where two streets meet; \"near\" picks one crossing) or [lon, lat]. Stages: floated (pitched, never adopted by a body that could build it), proposed (put forward by the TTC, the City or Metro, Metrolinx or the province, or voted into a plan), approved (funded, or its environmental assessment approved), building, building, open, closed, cancelled, dormant, superseded. Every event names its sources (keys of \"sources\"). Facts only: route, stations, dates, who and what happened, in our own words. No scanned plan maps are published.",
 "path": "ttc-history",
 "unlisted": false,
 "page": {
  "city": "Toronto",
  "title": "The subway Toronto built, and the one it almost did",
  "short": "Transit history",
  "description": "Every subway and LRT line Toronto opened, closed, cancelled or is still building, on one map with a timeline from 1946 to today.",
  "lede": "Drag the timeline to watch the network grow, and to see the lines that were promised, dug, cancelled or replaced along the way. Tap any line for its history and sources.",
  "note": "Unbuilt lines are traced by hand from published route descriptions and station lists, so they show where a line would have run, not its exact alignment. Built lines and lines under construction are drawn from the same data as the map.",
  "credits": "Built lines: TTC GTFS (City of Toronto Open Data) and OpenStreetMap contributors (ODbL). Streets and former municipalities: City of Toronto Open Data. Dates and routes: the sources listed on each line.",
  "start": 1946,
  "future": "Future",
  "later": {
   "title": "Not on this map yet",
   "lede": "Plans we know about but haven't drawn yet, usually because their routes are vague or they aren't subway or light rail. They'll come later.",
   "items": [
    [
     "1910–1912 streetcar subways",
     "Tunnels for streetcars under Yonge, Queen and Bloor. Voters turned down the spending in 1912."
    ],
    [
     "The Queen subway, 1959–1972",
     "A heavy-rail line under Queen in a string of Metro and TTC reports. We'll pick one plan to stand for them."
    ],
    [
     "GO-Urban, 1972–1974",
     "Ontario's three automated lines across the region. Only the Scarborough RT came out of it."
    ],
    [
     "Downtown Rapid Transit, early 1980s",
     "An automated, Scarborough RT-style version of the relief line to Union, studied just before Network 2011."
    ],
    [
     "OneCity, 2012",
     "TTC chair Karen Stintz and a fellow councillor proposed a network of new lines paid for by a property-tax increase; council dropped it within weeks."
    ],
    [
     "GO, SmartTrack and streetcars",
     "Regional rail, SmartTrack (2014) and streetcar lines such as Waterfront West are outside this map for now."
    ]
   ]
  }
 },
